(window.LADDER_CARDS=window.LADDER_CARDS||{})["7·econ"]=[{"q":"You cannot know whether she tells. What licenses splitting on “she tells”?","a":"the either-way principle","w":["finite-search","a promise","the stuck-pair technique"],"why":"She tells or she does not; reason in each case (7e)."},
 {"q":"Telling gives 5 < 10 if she tells, and 0 < 1 if she is silent. What technique concludes?","a":"better-either-way","w":["stuck-pair","proof by contradiction","same change to both sides"],"why":"Better in every case of her choice is better whatever she does."},
 {"q":"A pair where neither player does better by changing their own choice alone. What is it called on this page?","a":"stuck","w":["best for both","better either way","enforced"],"why":"(tell, tell) is stuck: a switch alone takes either from 5 years to 10."},
 {"q":"(silent, silent) gives each 1 year. Is it stuck?","a":"no: you gain by telling alone, 0 < 1","w":["yes: it is best for both","yes: nobody wants 10 years","no: she gains by staying silent"],"why":"Suppose it stuck; your switch to telling collides with that."},
 {"q":"Telling is better either way, so (tell, tell) is best. What is wrong?","a":"it is best for each alone; both do better at (silent, silent)","w":["telling is not better either way","(tell, tell) is not stuck","nothing: it is best"],"why":"5 years each, where 1 each was possible: the trap is in the table."},
 {"q":"Gas stations: cut gives 600 > 400 if the other keeps, 200 > 100 if the other cuts. Where do they land?","a":"(cut, cut), 200 each","w":["(keep, keep), 400 each","(cut, keep), 600 and 100","nowhere: no pair is stuck"],"why":"Better-either-way for each, then stuck-pair."},
 {"q":"Penalty c added where a player tells. Silence wins if 1 < c and 5 + c > 10. What bound is enough?","a":"c > 5","w":["c > 1","c > 10","c = 5"],"why":"Both cases at once take the larger bound."},
 {"q":"“We promised, so silence is safe.” What is wrong?","a":"a promise with no cost leaves the table as it was","w":["promises are never kept","silence is not better for both","either-way breaks promises"],"why":"Telling is still better whatever she does, so the promise breaks."},
 {"q":"Fewer years in one case, so telling is better either way. What is wrong?","a":"it must be better in every case","w":["one case is enough","the cases are not decidable","years are not fractions"],"why":"Better-either-way needs the conclusion in both cases."},
 {"q":"Played round after round, which simple rule did best in tournaments?","a":"silent at first, then copy the other’s last move","w":["always tell","always stay silent","tell at first, then copy"],"why":"The future is a cost: a teller today can be punished tomorrow."}];
