(window.LADDER_CARDS["11·econ"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["11·econ"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"11·econ.1.1","q":"What are demand and supply on this page, and where do the model's meet?","a":"D(p) the boxes buyers would take and S(p) the boxes growers would send at the price p; at $20 a box, 4000 boxes","w":["the price buyers pay and the price growers ask; at 4000 dollars","D(p) the boxes sent and S(p) the boxes taken; at $26","two constants; they never meet"],"why":"Both are functions of the price. 6000 − 100p = 1000 + 150p gives 250p = 5000."},
 {"u":"11·econ.2.1","q":"What does the meeting technique say, and why is what is sold a glued function?","a":"two linear functions with rates of opposite signs meet at exactly one price; what is sold is the lesser of the two, supply below p* and demand above","w":["two linear functions always meet twice; what is sold is their sum","demand and supply meet at every price; what is sold is supply","they meet at exactly one price; what is sold is the greater of the two"],"why":"p* = (k − c)/(b + d). The two pieces arrive at one height at 20, a joint with rates 150 and −100, and the most is sold there."},
 {"u":"11·econ.3.1","q":"What does a price floor do, and what does the rot technique say?","a":"below p* nothing, above it the price is the floor and boxes are left over; the boxes left over are 0 up to p* and S(f) − D(f) above, continuous with a joint at p*","w":["it raises what is sold; the leftover jumps at p*","it lowers the price; nothing is left over","above p* it leaves boxes over; the leftover is the same at every floor"],"why":"At $26, 3400 boxes are bought and 4900 sent, so 1500 are left. R(f) = 250f − 5000 above 20, and both pieces give 0 at 20."},
 {"u":"11·econ.4.1","q":"Why would growers want a floor, and why does it need a committee?","a":"takings T(f) = f · D(f) rise to $90 000 at a floor of $30, though fewer boxes sell; each grower alone would rather sell his leftover boxes for less, so the floor must be kept","w":["a floor sells more boxes; a committee counts them","takings fall with a floor; a committee pays the difference","takings rise without limit as the floor rises; a committee sets the highest floor"],"why":"T(f) = 90 000 − 100(f − 30)², by completing the square. If many growers undercut, the floor collapses."},
 {"u":"11·econ.5.1","q":"How does a picking cost make a jump, and why is there then no meeting price?","a":"below $8 a grower picks none and above it all 7000: a jump of 7000 at 8; supply is below demand under 8 and above it over 8","w":["supply climbs a box at a time past $8; they meet at $8","demand jumps at $8; they meet twice","the crop is too small; demand is always the greater"],"why":"D(8) = 5200 < 7000. E jumps from negative to positive at 8 without taking the value 0, and 1800 boxes stay on the trees."},
 {"u":"11·econ.6.1","q":"Why is “at a floor of $26, 4900 boxes are sold” a move to watch out for?","a":"what is sold is the lesser: D(26) = 3400; the other 1500 are sent and left over","w":["4900 is the number bought","no boxes are sold at a floor","what is sold is the sum, 8300"],"why":"Buyers cannot be made to take boxes, and growers answer the higher price by sending more."},
 {"u":"11·econ.7.1","q":"Handed p*, q* and the two rates, how do you write D and S?","a":"D(p) = q* − b(p − p*) and S(p) = q* + d(p − p*)","w":["D(p) = q* + b · p and S(p) = q* − d · p","D(p) = b(p − q*) and S(p) = d(p − q*)","D(p) = p* − b · q* and S(p) = p* + d · q*"],"why":"Both pass through (p*, q*), one falling at the rate b and the other rising at the rate d: 9e's linear function from a point and a rate."});
/* end phase F und_r11 */
/* phase F und_r12: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["12·econ"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["12·econ"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"12·econ.1.1","q":"What is the gap E(p), and what do two readings of it settle for the crab dock?","a":"E(p) = S(p) − D(p), the pounds offered less the pounds wanted at the price p; E(4) = −1200 and E(5) = 2250, so the gap changes sign between $4 and $5, with no jumps on the way","w":["E(p) = S(p) + D(p), the pounds sold; the readings give the price exactly","E(p) = D(p)/S(p); the readings show supply is a line","E(p) is the price itself; the readings show $4.50 matches"],"why":"E = 50p³ + 400p − 6000 has degree 3, and the ladder has no formula for its zero. A sign change is still enough to start."},
 {"u":"12·econ.2.1","q":"What does matching-price say, and what does it spend?","a":"if S and D are continuous on [v, w], with S below D at v and above D at w, some price strictly between has S(p) = D(p); it spends in-between-value on the gap E = S − D, and so the line with no holes, once","w":["supply and demand always cross; it spends nothing","the match is at the middle price; it spends bisection","if supply rises and demand falls there are two matches; it spends the quadratic formula"],"why":"E is continuous as a difference of continuous functions and changes sign. The proof solves nothing: it says a match exists."},
 {"u":"12·econ.3.1","q":"How does halving find where the match is, and how tight is the trap after seven halvings?","a":"test the gap at the middle of the trap and keep the half where its sign still changes; from [4, 5], seven halvings leave a width of 1/128 of a dollar, under a cent","w":["average the two end prices once; the trap is then exact","raise the price a cent at a time; seven steps give seven cents","solve E(p) = 0 by formula; no trap is needed"],"why":"Halvings-width gives 1/2⁷. The price is about $4.39 a pound, where about 4240 pounds a day change hands."},
 {"u":"12·econ.4.1","q":"What does one-match say, and why can a fishery have two matches?","a":"when supply only rises and demand only falls there is at most one matching price; over many seasons a fishery's supply rises and then turns back down, so the gap changes sign twice","w":["there is always exactly one match; a fishery has two docks","there are at most two matches; a fishery has two kinds of crab","no match exists when supply rises; a fishery sets two prices by rule"],"why":"A rising gap cannot be 0 twice. With S_L = 2400p − 200p² the matches are near $2.64 and $11.36, and the proof does not say which a market settles at."},
 {"u":"12·econ.5.1","q":"What does the proof leave unsaid about whether a market gets to the match?","a":"everything about the road: that a price drifts toward the match is a law read from markets, not a technique, and a proof that a price exists is not a proof that anyone finds it","w":["nothing: halving is what a market does","only the number of traders","only the units of the price"],"why":"The technique says a match exists. How long the road takes, and whether the market overshoots, is not in it."},
 {"u":"12·econ.6.1","q":"Why does the dock clear near the match and not at it?","a":"prices come in whole cents, a line with holes: at $4.39 buyers are short and at $4.40 crab is left over, so no price on the list matches","w":["the boats round their catch to whole pounds","the match is at a negative price","buyers never pay the posted price"],"why":"As for 12d's fractions, in-between-value fails where the least upper bound is missing. The matching price lives on the line, not on the price list."},
 {"u":"12·econ.7.1","q":"Why is “E(v) < 0 and E(w) < 0, so no match between” a move to watch out for?","a":"equal signs at the two ends promise nothing either way: the gap can rise above 0 and come back, as the long-run fishery's does","w":["a gap is never negative at both ends","equal signs prove there are exactly two matches","the technique needs E(v) = E(w)"],"why":"In-between-value speaks only when the signs differ. For equal signs, test prices inside until a sign changes, or prove there is none."},
 {"u":"12·econ.8.1","q":"Handed a table of the gap at a few prices, what does it prove?","a":"each sign change between neighbouring prices holds a match, so the table proves at least that many matching prices, if the gap has no jumps","w":["exactly as many matches as it has rows","the matching price itself, at the smallest gap in the table","nothing, since a table is not a formula"],"why":"Each pair of neighbours with opposite signs is a trap. There may be more matches between rows of the same sign."});
/* end phase F und_r12 */
