(window.LADDER_CARDS["13·econ"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["13·econ"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"13·econ.1.1","q":"What are the cost, the average cost and the marginal cost on this page?","a":"the cost C(q) is what the day costs as a function of the units made; the average cost is C(q)/q; the marginal cost is the slope C′(q), what one more costs, read as a rate","w":["the cost is the price of one unit; the average cost is C(q) · q; the marginal cost is the fixed cost","the cost is the fixed cost alone; the average cost is the slope; the marginal cost is C(q)/q","the cost is the revenue; the average cost is the profit per unit; the marginal cost is the cost of the first unit"],"why":"For C(q) = 600 + 2q + 0.004q² the marginal cost is 2 + 0.008q by polynomial-slope. The fixed cost F = C(0) is paid whatever q is."},
 {"u":"13·econ.2.1","q":"What does next-one say, and why is the next jar cheap early and dear late?","a":"for a cost F + kq + bq² the next unit costs exactly the marginal cost half a unit along, C′(q + ½); early the fixed cost is spread thin so the average is dear and the next jar cheap, and late the next jar carries all the strain","w":["the next unit costs exactly the average; early and late the next jar costs the same","the next unit costs C′(q) exactly; early the next jar is dear because the fixed cost is in it","the next unit costs the fixed cost; late the next jar is cheap because the kitchen is warm"],"why":"C(q + 1) − C(q) = k + b(2q + 1) = C′(q + ½), which misses C′(q) by exactly b. At 200 jars the next costs 3.604 and the average is 5.80."},
 {"u":"13·econ.3.1","q":"What does marginal-against-average say, and why is it the rule of any average?","a":"the average cost falls while the next unit costs less than the average and rises while it costs more, since A′(q) = (C′(q) − A(q))/q; a new value below an average pulls it down, and one above pulls it up","w":["the average cost always falls as more is made; a new value never moves an average","the average cost equals the marginal cost at every q; the two are one function","the average cost rises while the next unit costs less; a low value pulls an average up"],"why":"Quotient for slopes on C(q)/q. A student whose next mark is below her average pulls the average down. The average is level exactly where C′(q) = A(q)."},
 {"u":"13·econ.4.1","q":"Why do the first units lose money, and where are the break-even points?","a":"the fixed cost must be paid before any unit is sold, and it takes many units to pay it back; for the salsa maker at $6 a jar the profit is 0 at about 184 and 816 jars a day","w":["the first units cost more to make than the later ones; break-even is at 0 jars","the price is lower for the first units; break-even is at 500 jars only","nothing is lost on the first units; break-even is at every q"],"why":"P(q) = −0.004q² + 4q − 600 is −600 at 0. The quadratic formula gives q = 500 ± √100 000."},
 {"u":"13·econ.5.1","q":"What is the marginal revenue, and what does profit-of-one-more say?","a":"the slope of the revenue q · p(q), which is p + q · p′; making one more adds to profit just past q when the marginal revenue is above the marginal cost there, and takes from it when it is below","w":["the price of the next unit; one more adds to profit whenever its price is above 0","the revenue divided by the units sold; one more adds to profit when the average cost is falling","the slope of the price; one more always adds to profit"],"why":"When the price must fall to sell more, one more sale brings in less than its price, since it lowers the price of all the others. P′ = R′ − C′ by slopes-add."},
 {"u":"13·econ.6.1","q":"Why is “every unit sold at a profit, so the day makes a profit” a move to watch out for?","a":"the fixed cost is paid before any unit is sold, so a day can lose money though each unit sells for more than it took to make","w":["no unit can be sold for more than it took to make","profit is counted per unit only, never per day","the day's profit is the profit of the last unit sold"],"why":"At $6 a jar each early jar earns about $4 over its own cost, yet below about 184 jars the day still loses: the $600 is not yet paid back."},
 {"u":"13·econ.7.1","q":"Handed a marginal cost and a fixed cost, how do you recover a cost, and what is left open?","a":"find a cost whose slope is the marginal cost and whose value at 0 is the fixed cost, and check it by taking the slope; whether it is the only one is left open","w":["multiply the marginal cost by q and stop; nothing is left open","add the fixed cost to the marginal cost; the slope is left open","divide the marginal cost by q; the fixed cost is left open"],"why":"2 + 0.008q and 600 give 600 + 2q + 0.004q². That no other cost fits is 14e's question."});
/* end phase F und_r13 */
/* phase F und_r14: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["14·econ"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["14·econ"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"14·econ.1.1","q":"Where is profit greatest, and what else must be checked?","a":"inside the range, where the slope of revenue meets the slope of cost and the second slope of profit is negative; the ends are candidates too, making nothing and running flat out","w":["where revenue is greatest; nothing else","where cost is least; the price","where the average cost meets the price; the fixed cost"],"why":"G′ = R′ − C′, and flat-top makes it 0 at a top inside. Make one more unit while it brings in more than it costs."},
 {"u":"14·econ.2.1","q":"What rule does a grower who takes the price follow, and why stop at 50 tons?","a":"make where the price equals the slope of cost; at 50 tons the next ton costs about $101 to grow, more than the $100 it sells for","w":["make where the average cost equals the price; at 50 tons the average cost is $100","make as much as the field allows; at 50 tons the field is full","make where the cost is least; at 50 tons the cost is 0"],"why":"G′(y) = 100 − 2y is 0 at 50. The average cost there is $80 a ton, well below the price, but the next ton is what decides."},
 {"u":"14·econ.3.1","q":"Why does a seller alone make less and charge more than many sellers would?","a":"it makes where the slope of revenue meets the slope of cost, and the slope of revenue is below the price, since selling one more lowers the price of every unit already sold","w":["its costs are higher than a small plant's","buyers want less from a seller alone","it makes where the price meets the slope of cost, as many sellers do"],"why":"R′(q) = p(q) + q · p′(q), with the second part negative. Many small plants cannot move the price, so they make where p = C′, which is more."},
 {"u":"14·econ.4.1","q":"Which taxes move the best output, and which do not?","a":"a tax on each unit moves it, because it changes the slope of cost; a fixed tax or a share of profit does not, though a fixed tax can still close a plant","w":["every tax moves it by the same amount","only a fixed tax moves it","no tax moves it"],"why":"A fixed tax has slope 0, and a constant factor leaves a level point level. With $5 a ton here, output falls from 800 to 700 and buyers pay $2 of each $5."},
 {"u":"14·econ.5.1","q":"Why is “a higher fixed cost lowers the best output” a move to watch out for?","a":"a constant has slope 0, so it leaves the slope of cost and the best output where they were; it decides only whether to open at all","w":["a higher fixed cost raises the best output","fixed costs change the price","a fixed cost is part of the slope of revenue"],"why":"The best output is where R′ = C′, and the fixed part of C is not in C′."});
/* end phase F und_r14 */
/* phase F und_r15: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["15·econ"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["15·econ"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"15·econ.1.1","q":"How does a balance grow at a yearly rate, and why is 6% added monthly more than 6%?","a":"each year multiplies it by 1 + i, so after n years it is (1 + i)ⁿ times the start; 0.5% a month multiplies by 1.005 twelve times, about 1.0617, since interest is paid on interest","w":["each year adds i times the start, so it grows by ni; monthly adding changes nothing","each year multiplies it by i; monthly adding gives 6% exactly","each year adds a fixed sum; monthly adding gives 0.5%"],"why":"Power-of-one-plus says (1 + i/12)¹² ≥ 1 + i always. The quoted rate and the rate really earned are two numbers."},
 {"u":"15·econ.2.1","q":"What is a payment A due in n years worth today, and why do present values add?","a":"A/(1 + i)ⁿ, the amount that would grow to A in n years; present values are amounts on the same day, so they add","w":["A · (1 + i)ⁿ; they add because every payment is equal","A − n · i; they do not add","A/n; they add only within one year"],"why":"$10 000 due in 6 years at 5% is worth about $7462 today. The price of a stream of payments is the sum of their present values."},
 {"u":"15·econ.3.1","q":"What is a run of n equal payments worth today, and what are payments forever worth?","a":"M(1 − (1 + j)⁻ⁿ)/j, by finite-ratio-sum with ratio 1/(1 + j); payments of A forever are worth A/i, by endless-ratio-sum","w":["n · M, the payments added; payments forever are worth nothing","M/(1 + j)ⁿ; payments forever have no total","M · j · n; payments forever are worth A · i"],"why":"Set the worth equal to the loan and solve for the payment. For $600 000 over 360 months at 0.5% a month, about $3597.30."},
 {"u":"15·econ.4.1","q":"What is a deal's true rate, and how is the pickup's found?","a":"the rate at which its payments are worth its price today; the worth of the 48 payments falls as the rate rises, so in-between-value gives exactly one rate, and halving traps it near 0.77% a month","w":["the extra paid divided by the price; one division gives it","the rate the dealer quotes; it is read off the contract","the rate at which the payments add to the price; it is 0"],"why":"A(0) = 48 > 40 and A(0.01) ≈ 37.97 < 40. Ten halvings give j ≈ 0.0077, about 9.6% a year."},
 {"u":"15·econ.5.1","q":"Why is “$1200 for $1100 cash, so the rate is 100/1100 ≈ 9%” a move to watch out for?","a":"the balance owed falls with each payment, so the $100 is interest on much less than $1100 for a year; the true rate is far higher","w":["the rate should be 100/1200","the extra $100 is not interest","the true rate is lower than 9%"],"why":"The true rate is the one at which the payments are worth the cash price. Here it is near 17.7% a year."});
/* end phase F und_r15 */
/* phase F und_r16: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["16·econ"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["16·econ"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"16·econ.1.1","q":"What are the demand and supply curves on this page, and where does the strawberry market trade?","a":"D(q) is the most the buyer of the q-th flat would pay and S(q) the least its seller would take; they meet at q* = 4 thousand flats and p* = $24","w":["D(q) is what buyers paid last week and S(q) what sellers asked; they meet at q* = 6 and p* = $4","D(q) is the least a buyer would pay and S(q) the most a seller would take; they meet at q* = 9","D(q) is the number of buyers and S(q) the number of sellers; they meet at p* = $40"],"why":"D − S = 36 − 5q − q² = −(q + 9)(q − 4). It is continuous and falls, so it is 0 once, at q = 4."},
 {"u":"16·econ.2.1","q":"What are the two gains as areas, and what does total-gain say?","a":"the buyers' gain is the area between the demand curve and the price, about 42.67, and the sellers' the area between the price and the supply curve, 40; trading exactly the market quantity makes the total as large as it can be","w":["the buyers' gain is p*q*, 96, and the sellers' the same; the total is greatest at the highest price","the buyers' gain is the area under D, and the sellers' the area under S; the total grows with every flat traded","the two gains are always equal; the total depends only on the price"],"why":"The total is ∫₀^q (D − S). Its slope D − S is + before q* and − after, so it is greatest at q*."},
 {"u":"16·econ.3.1","q":"What does lost-gain say a tax destroys, and what does it not?","a":"only the gain on the trades it stops, ∫ from q_t to q* of D − S; the tax raised is not destroyed, since it moves to the state","w":["everything it raises, t·q_t; the stopped trades cost nothing","the whole of the buyers' gain; the sellers lose nothing","nothing at all; a tax only moves money"],"why":"Buyers' gain, sellers' gain and tax raised add to G(q_t). So what is gone is G(q*) − G(q_t)."},
 {"u":"16·econ.4.1","q":"What does who-carries say, and does it matter who hands the money over?","a":"the side whose curve is steeper carries more of a tax, buyers bt/(b + d) and sellers dt/(b + d); it does not matter who hands it to the state","w":["the side that hands the money over carries all of it","the two sides always carry half each","the side whose curve is flatter carries more; collecting from buyers moves the whole tax to them"],"why":"The same tax collected from the other side gives the same q_t, p_b and p_s. In the model buyers carry $7 of $12."},
 {"u":"16·econ.5.1","q":"What does a price fixed above the market destroy beyond the stopped trades?","a":"the cost of growing fruit that is not sold: 2.4 thousand flats grown at a cost of 60 thousand dollars and left in the field","w":["nothing more: the stopped trades are the whole loss","the buyers' whole gain on the flats they still buy","the tax the state would have raised"],"why":"At $31 buyers take 3 thousand flats and sellers would grow 5.4. The cost is ∫₃^5.4 S(q) dq = 60."},
 {"u":"16·econ.6.1","q":"Why is “a tax destroys what it raises” a move to watch out for?","a":"what it raises is moved from buyers and sellers to the state; what it destroys is the gain on the trades that no longer happen","w":["a tax raises nothing in this model","what it raises is destroyed twice, once for each side","a tax destroys only the sellers' gain"],"why":"Lost-gain counts three parts that add to G(q_t). The tax raised is one of them, still there."});
